# Welcome to March 2, 2026

The Singularity just took its first incoming fire.

Unidentified "objects" [struck an Amazon Web Services data center in the United Arab Emirates](https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/amazon-cloud-unit-flags-issues-bahrain-uae-data-centers-amid-iran-strikes-2026-03-02/) on Sunday, triggering sparks and fire that [forced authorities to cut power to two availability zones](https://www.crn.com/news/cloud/2026/objects-strike-spark-fire-at-aws-data-center-in-the-middle-east), taking down S3, DynamoDB, and [over 60 core services](https://business.outlookindia.com/deeptech/aws-uae-data-center-hit-amid-iran-israel-war-regional-services-disrupted) across the Middle East region. AWS [won't say whether the strike was connected to Iran's retaliatory attacks](https://www.businessinsider.com/amazon-web-services-data-center-fire-objects-middle-east-strikes-2026-3), but the timing leaves little to the imagination — the facility went dark hours after Iranian missiles and drones [hit airports, ports, and residential towers across the Gulf](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/02/us-iran-live-updates-trump-oil-gold.html). Rest of World [called it the end of the Gulf's pitch](https://restofworld.org/2026/amazon-uae-data-center-fire-iran-strike/) as a safe harbor for the world's data. CSIS had already warned that [if compute is the new oil, war in the Gulf raises the stakes](https://www.csis.org/analysis/if-compute-new-oil-war-gulf-significantly-raises-stakes) beyond anything the hyperscalers priced in. For the first time in history, Big Tech infrastructure is a direct casualty of kinetic military action.

**The war is widening faster than the acronyms can track.** QatarEnergy [halted all liquefied natural gas production](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/2/qatarenergy-worlds-largest-lng-firm-halts-production-after-iran-attacks) after Iranian drones struck facilities in Ras Laffan and Mesaieed, sending European gas futures [up nearly 50%](https://www.dailysabah.com/business/energy/european-gas-prices-spike-nearly-50-as-qatar-halts-lng-output/amp). Saudi Aramco [shut down its Ras Tanura refinery](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-02/saudi-arabia-s-ras-tanura-refinery-shuts-down-after-drone-attack), the kingdom's largest, after drone shrapnel ignited a fire. Brent crude [touched $82 a barrel](https://www.gulf-times.com/article/721645/business/oil-prices-surge-7), its highest since January 2025. Kuwaiti air defenses [shot down three U.S. F-15E Strike Eagles](https://www.defensenews.com/news/your-military/2026/03/02/3-f-15s-shot-down-by-kuwait-in-friendly-fire-incident-pilots-safe-us-says/) in a friendly fire incident — all six crew ejected safely. Four American service members [are now confirmed killed](https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-us-war-day-3-american-deaths-israel-gulf-allies-hit-missile-strikes/). Trump told the New York Post he [won't rule out ground troops](https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-us-war-day-3-american-deaths-israel-gulf-allies-hit-missile-strikes/) and suggested the operation could last "four weeks or less." Congress [is set to vote on a bipartisan war powers resolution](https://www.nhpr.org/2026-03-02/congress-gears-up-for-vote-on-trumps-war-powers-in-iran-after-the-battle-began) this week, though previous efforts to assert authority over undeclared wars have a perfect record of failure.

**The Pentagon's new AI contractor is already under the microscope.** The Verge published a detailed analysis arguing [OpenAI caved to the Pentagon on surveillance](https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/887309/openai-anthropic-dod-military-pentagon-contract-sam-altman-hegseth) — that Altman's claim his contract prohibits mass domestic surveillance rests on [compliance with Executive Order 12333](https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/887309/openai-anthropic-dod-military-pentagon-contract-sam-altman-hegseth), a Reagan-era directive that civil liberties advocates have spent decades arguing [does not say what the government claims it does](https://www.theregister.com/2026/03/02/open_ai_dow_anthropic_ai_ethics/). Altman ran his Saturday AMA on X [calling the Anthropic blacklisting "an extremely scary precedent"](https://x.com/sama/status/2027957684625150444) while simultaneously defending the deal he signed to replace them. The Wall Street Journal reported Altman [acknowledged the optics were "rushed."](https://trib.al/RoNSEqK) The company that copied Anthropic's red lines got a classified contract; the company that wrote them got the Huawei treatment; and the fine print may not say what either side claims.

**China's answer to the chip embargo drops this week.** DeepSeek V4 is [expected to launch by March 4](https://technode.com/2026/03/02/deepseek-plans-v4-multimodal-model-release-this-week-sources-say/), timed to land before China's parliamentary sessions — a [trillion-parameter mixture-of-experts model](https://awesomeagents.ai/models/deepseek-v4/) with native multimodal generation across text, image, video, and audio, a one-million-token context window, and full optimization for [Huawei Ascend and Cambricon chips](https://recodechinaai.substack.com/p/deepseeks-next-move-what-v4-will). DeepSeek [did not share V4 with Nvidia or AMD](https://www.aol.com/news/deepseek-release-long-awaited-ai-162425973.html), breaking standard industry practice. Leaked benchmarks suggest [frontier-competitive performance](https://awesomeagents.ai/models/deepseek-v4/) at a fraction of Western inference costs. The global AI supply chain didn't just fork — it shipped.

**The autonomous fleet crossed the profitability line.** Pony.ai announced its Gen-7 Robotaxi achieved [positive monthly operating profit per vehicle in Shenzhen](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/03/02/3246991/0/en/PONY-AI-Inc-Achieved-Gen-7-Robotaxi-UE-Breakeven-in-Shenzhen-Strengthening-Path-to-Scalable-Commercialization.html), averaging [338 yuan daily net revenue across 23 orders per vehicle](https://cnevpost.com/2026/03/02/pony-ai-hits-unit-profitability-in-2nd-chinese-city/) — its second Chinese megacity after Guangzhou. Meanwhile, Waymo is reportedly [buying 50,000 Hyundai Ioniq 5s for $2.5 billion](https://electrek.co/2026/02/11/hyundai-supply-waymo-50000-ioniq-5-robotaxis/) to fuel expansion to twenty cities by year-end, though the SF Standard notes the Ioniq 5 has a [well-documented ICCU failure problem](https://sfstandard.com/2026/03/02/robot-turtles-waymo-s-next-gen-cars-could-buggy-hyundai-evs/) that leaves cars undriveable. The self-driving car just reached unit economics; now it has to reach the dealership for parts.

**Apple opened its "big week" with the consumer economy's finest counterargument.** The [iPhone 17e launched at $599](https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/03/apple-introduces-iphone-17e/) with an A19 chip, MagSafe, 48MP Fusion camera, and 256GB base storage — doubling the previous generation. The [iPad Air got M4](https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/03/apple-introduces-the-new-ipad-air-powered-by-m4/) with 12GB unified memory and Wi-Fi 7 at the same $599 starting price. CNBC reports a [$699 budget MacBook](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/02/apple-iphone-17e-ipad-air-m4-chip.html) is expected later this week. Cupertino is still shipping cameras and keyboards while the intelligence economy [decides which model gets the nuclear codes](https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/887309/openai-anthropic-dod-military-pentagon-contract-sam-altman-hegseth).

The data center took a missile, the refinery took a drone, the gas market took 50%, and the friendly fire took three jets — but at least the new iPhone has MagSafe.
